.TH ROGUE 6 "1 April 1981"
.UC 4
.SH NAME
rogue \- Exploring The Dungeons of Doom
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rogue
[
.I save_file
|
.B -r
|
.B -s
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
.I Rogue
is a computer fantasy game with a new twist.  It is crt oriented and the
object of the game is to survive the attacks of various monsters and get
a lot of gold, rather than the puzzle solving orientation of most computer
fantasy games.
.PP
To get started you really only need to know two commands.  The command
.B ?
will give you a list of the available commands and the command
.B /
will identify the things you see on the screen.
.PP
To win the game (as opposed to merely playing to beat other people high
scores) you must locate the Amulet of Yendor which is somewhere below
the 20th level of the dungeon and get it out.  Nobody has achieved this
yet and if somebody does, they will probably go down in history as a hero
among heroes.
.PP
When the game ends, either by your death, when you quit, or if you (by
some miracle) manage to win,
.I rogue
will give you a list of the top-ten scorers.  The scoring is based entirely
upon how much gold you get.  There is a 10% penalty for getting yourself
killed.
.PP
For more detailed directions, read the supplied Markdown guides
.B rogue.md
(English) and
.B rogue_jp.md
(Japanese).
.SH FILES
.PP
This macOS/Linux build lets the executable name and related file names be selected
at build time.  The recommended method is to run the supplied
.B configure
script before
.BR make .

.PP
A normal configured build is:
.PP
.RS
.nf
./configure
make
.fi
.RE
.PP
The configure script detects a C compiler and a usable curses library.  It
tries
.BR ncursesw ,
.BR ncurses ,
and then
.B curses
in that order.
.PP
After building, the program, man page, and documents can be installed with:
.PP
.RS
.nf
./configure --program=rogue52 --prefix=/usr/local
make
make install
.fi
.RE
.PP
The default install layout is:
.PP
.RS
.nf
/usr/local/bin/rogue52
/usr/local/share/man/man6/rogue52.6
/usr/local/share/doc/rogue52/README.md
/usr/local/share/doc/rogue52/README_mac_linux_ja.md
/usr/local/share/doc/rogue52/LICENSE.TXT
/usr/local/share/doc/rogue52/rogue.md
/usr/local/share/doc/rogue52/rogue_jp.md
.fi
.RE
.PP
The install locations can be changed with
.BR --prefix ,
.BR --bindir ,
.BR --mandir ,
and
.BR --docdir .
For package builds,
.B DESTDIR
is supported:
.PP
.RS
.nf
make DESTDIR=/tmp/package-root install
.fi
.RE
.PP
Installed files for the current configuration can be removed with:
.PP
.RS
.nf
make uninstall
.fi
.RE
.PP
The install target does not create save or score files themselves.
If
.B --file-dir=DIR
was used with configure,
.B make install
creates that directory so the runtime files can be written there later.
Directories named only by
.BR --savedir ,
.BR --savefile ,
or
.B --scorefile
are not created automatically.
.PP
The executable name and file paths can be configured with:
.PP
.RS
.nf
./configure --program=rogue52
./configure --program=rogue52 --savedir=.
./configure --program=rogue52 --file-dir=.
./configure --program=rogue52 --savedir='~/Documents'
./configure --program=rogue52 --file-dir='~/Documents'
./configure --program=rogue52 --savefile='./rogue52.save'
./configure --program=rogue52 --scorefile='./rogue52.scr'
.fi
.RE
.PP
The
.B --file-dir
option sets the default save directory and, unless
.B --scorefile
is specified, places the score file in the same directory.  An explicit
.B --savedir
or
.B --savefile
setting takes precedence for saved games; an explicit
.B --scorefile
setting takes precedence for scores.  A leading
.B ~/
is expanded to the user's home directory.
When
.B --file-dir=DIR
is configured,
.B make install
creates
.I DIR
only.  It does not create the save or score files.

.PP
Wizard mode can be enabled at configure time with:
.PP
.RS
.nf
./configure --enable-wizardmode
.fi
.RE
.PP
The direct make variables described below remain available for manual builds.

.PP
By default,
.PP
.RS
.nf
make
.fi
.RE
.PP
builds the program as
.B rogue
and uses the following files.  The save file is relative to the current
directory when the game is run:
.PP
.RS
.nf
./rogue.save
rogue.scr
.fi
.RE
.PP
Specifying
.B PROG
or
.B PROGRAM
changes the executable name and, unless overridden, the default save and score
file names.  If both are specified,
.B PROGRAM
takes precedence:
.PP
.RS
.nf
make PROG=rogue52
.fi
.RE
.PP
uses:
.PP
.RS
.nf
./rogue52.save
rogue52.scr
.fi
.RE
.PP
The default save directory can be changed with
.BR SAVEDIR :
.PP
.RS
.nf
make PROG=rogue52 SAVEDIR=.
make PROG=rogue52 SAVEDIR='~/Documents'
make PROG=rogue52 SAVEDIR=/tmp
.fi
.RE
.PP
These produce default save files such as:
.PP
.RS
.nf
./rogue52.save
~/Documents/rogue52.save
/tmp/rogue52.save
.fi
.RE
.PP
The save file itself can also be specified directly with
.BR SAVEFILE :
.PP
.RS
.nf
make PROG=rogue52 SAVEFILE=./rogue52.save
make PROG=rogue52 SAVEFILE='~/Documents/rogue52.save'
make PROG=rogue52 SAVEFILE=/tmp/rogue52.save
.fi
.RE
.PP
If
.B SAVEFILE
contains a slash, it is treated as a path and is not appended to
.B SAVEDIR
or another directory.  By default,
.B SAVEDIR
is
.BR . ,
so the save file is relative to the current directory when the game is run.  A leading
.B ~/
is expanded to the user's home directory by
.I rogue
at run time.
.PP
The score file path can be changed with
.BR SCOREFILE :
.PP
.RS
.nf
make PROG=rogue52 SCOREFILE=/tmp/rogue52.scr
.fi
.RE
.PP
Relative score file paths are relative to the current directory when the game
is run.  Concurrent score updates are protected by operating-system locks on
the score file itself; no separate
.B .lck
file is created.  The legacy configure option
.B --lockfile
is accepted for compatibility but ignored.

.PP
Wizard mode can be enabled at build time with:
.PP
.RS
.nf
make wizard
.fi
.RE
.PP
This builds the game with
.B -DWIZARD
enabled.  Press
.B Ctrl-P
during the game to enable wizard mode.
.PP
The
.B wizard
target accepts the same build-time file-name options, for example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
make wizard PROG=rogue52
make wizard PROG=rogue52 SAVEDIR=.
make wizard PROG=rogue52 SAVEFILE='~/Documents/rogue52.save'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Extra preprocessor options can also be appended with
.BR EXTRA_CPPFLAGS ,
for example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
make EXTRA_CPPFLAGS='-DWIZARD'
.fi
.RE

.SH SEE ALSO
Michael C. Toy,
.I "A Guide to the Dungeons of Doom"
(included as rogue.md and rogue_jp.md)
.SH BUGS
.PP
Probably infinite.  Currently known bugs are: Sometimes you are still hungry
even after you eat food and sometimes you get a monster on the screen in
reverse video which may or may not cause a core dump.

This version preserves UTF-8 bytes in string options, so the player name and
favorite fruit may be specified as UTF-8 text through ROGUEOPTS, for example
.B ROGUEOPTS='name=Blue Meanie,fruit=apple' .
The in-game option screen also accepts UTF-8 text for string options such as
Name, Fruit, and Save file.  Backspace, Delete, Left, and Right edit by UTF-8
character boundaries, and Japanese characters are treated as double-width for
cursor placement in the option input field.  IME pre-edit and conversion
display is handled by the terminal emulator; confirmed UTF-8 text is then
handled safely by Rogue.  The startup greeting and score list display the
stored player name as UTF-8.
